import BlueprintPage from '../components/BlueprintPage.astro'; const sections=[
{title:'Therapy approaches',paragraphs:['Therapy may draw on CBT-informed, behavioural, supportive, mindfulness, family, or other approaches according to the concern, clinician, and person’s goals. “Evidence-informed” does not mean that one method works for every person or that a result is guaranteed. A clinician considers the concern, available evidence, professional training, safety and the patient’s preferences when discussing an approach.','CBT-informed work may help someone notice links among situations, thoughts, feelings and actions. Behavioural approaches may focus on patterns and practical changes. Supportive therapy may help a person make sense of current pressures and strengthen coping. Family or couples work may address shared communication and relationship patterns when suitable. The names are broad descriptions; ask the clinician what sessions actually involve.']},
{title:'How to decide whether an approach fits',paragraphs:['Start with the change you hope to make. You might want to understand recurring worry, respond differently during conflict, manage a transition, or cope with a difficult experience. Ask how the clinician would work toward that goal, what participation involves and how progress would be discussed. You can ask about alternatives, likely time commitments, fee and how to raise concerns if a session feels unhelpful.','A good plan should be understandable and revisited. Goals may change as you learn what helps. If a suggested technique increases distress or does not fit your circumstances, tell the clinician so the plan can be reviewed. Therapy should not be presented as a guaranteed cure or as the only option for every mental-health concern.']},
{title:'Clinical mindfulness',paragraphs:['Mindfulness skills may support attention or coping with performance and exam stress, but they do not replace assessment or emergency care. A brief exercise may involve noticing breath, sounds or physical sensations; a person should be able to stop if it feels uncomfortable. Mindfulness is not a test of concentration and does not require someone to ignore a problem or remain in a distressing situation.','Students and families can also explore the <a href="/resources">self-guided tools and resources</a>. These are educational exercises rather than a clinical treatment plan. If distress persists, affects functioning or raises safety concerns, consider a professional conversation.']},
{title:'How therapy relates to psychiatry',paragraphs:['Therapy is psychological support provided by a trained professional within their scope. Psychiatry is medical care that may include assessment of physical and mental health and discussion of medication. Some people use both, while others start with one service or need a different referral. The right route depends on the concern and the person. Read about <a href="/therapy">clinical psychology and therapy</a>, <a href="/psychiatry">psychiatry consultations</a> and the <a href="/psychiatrist-vs-psychologist">care-role comparison</a>.','At Mind Grace, ask about current clinician availability and fees before booking. Review <a href="/fees">fees</a> and <a href="/process">first-visit information</a>. A request is not a confirmed appointment until the clinic agrees a time. For immediate danger, call 112; Mind Grace is not an emergency service.']}
];
